 \begin{figure}[htb!] % between [] you put options e.g. ht = here top
% \hspace*{-0.5cm}
 %\hspace*{-2cm}
\centering
%\rule{12.8cm}{7.2cm}
%\setlength{\abovecaptionskip}{5pt}
\makebox[\textwidth]{\includegraphics[width=0.7\paperwidth]{figure/NplRatios-1}}
%\vspace{-1cm}}
\caption{\label{figure:NplRatios}NPL: Distribution per Year}
 %\vspace*{-3mm}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[htb!] % between [] you put options e.g. ht = here top
% \hspace*{-0.5cm}
 %\hspace*{-2cm}
\centering
%\rule{12.8cm}{7.2cm}
%\setlength{\abovecaptionskip}{5pt}
\makebox[\textwidth]{\includegraphics[width=0.5\paperwidth]{figure/npl.summ}}
\caption{NPL: Distribution-Basic Information}
\label{npl.summ}
%\vspace{-1cm}}

 %\vspace*{-3mm}
\end{figure}

Figure \ref{figure:NplRatios} shows the distribution in the sample of non-performing loans per exposure type for the period 2014-2019. A decline in non-performing loans over the years is observable,  with  NPL ratios ranging between 0\% and 13\% . Moreover, there are many banks showing high NPL ratios over the 75th quantile, suggesting the presence of  heterogeneity across observations and a positive (right) skewness of the NPL distribution. Furthermore, there are important disparities in NPL when comparing exposures types as observed Table \ref{npl.summ}.  In particular, the average NPL ratio for non-financial corporations stood  at 12,3\%, compared with 6,9\% for households and 8,3 \% for financial corporations other than credit institutions for the period 2014-2019. Comparatively, the exposure type financial corporations other than credit institutions shows the greatest number of observations  with NPL classified as outliers in the sample. Moreover, non-financial corporations have the highest NPL on average and the largest distance between the lower (25th) and the upper quantile (75th). Interestingly, Table \ref{npl.summ} also contains skewness and kurtosis metrics for the sample. In this regard, the value for the skewness, which measured the degree of symmetry in the distribution and the relative size of the tails, is large and positive  for all exposure types, confirming the presence of positive (right) skewness and non-symmetry in the distribution. In addition, the value for the kurtosis, which measures the degree of flatness in the distribution, is similarly large and positive for all exposure types, signaling the presence of a tail-heavy and slightly flat distribution. Given the presence of outliers and the non-normal distribution of NPL, the additional use of quantile regression  methodology seems reasonable. Similar conclusions are provided observing  the density and Quantile-Quantile diagrams in Figure \ref{NplRatios1}.

% The Figure \ref{figure:NplRatios} shows the distribution in the sample of non-perfoming loans per exposure type for the period 2014-2019. A decline in non-performing loans over the years is visible with  a npl rations  between 0\% and 10\% in the median,also the present of many banks showing  NPL ratios over the 75th quantile is clear, suggesting the present of  very heterogeneous NPLs across observations and a  positive (right) skewness of the NPL distribution.Comparatively,the exposure type "financial corporations other than credit institutes"  shows the highest NPL ratios in the sample  and  greater dispersion reaching values around 20\%-30\%  for the 75th quantile. 

