\begin{figure}[hbt] % between [] you put options e.g. ht = here top
% \hspace*{-0.5cm}
 %\hspace*{-2cm}
\centering
%\rule{12.8cm}{7.2cm}
%\setlength{\abovecaptionskip}{5pt}
\makebox[\textwidth]{\includegraphics[width=0.6\paperwidth]{figure/ExposuresRatio-1}}
%\vspace{-1cm}}

 %\vspace*{-3mm}
 \caption{Exposures Ratio Distribution: EU \& Domestic}
 \label{ExposuresRatio}
\end{figure}

Figure \ref{ExposuresRatio} shows the distribution in the sample of  original exposures in the bank's country of origin and original exposures with destination in an  EU country in relation to the bank's total original exposures. Although the average is around 75\% of total original exposures  both in homecountry and EU exposures, the distribution  tends to be  right skewed  and slightly flat for homecountry exposure ratios. In the case of EU ratios, the distribution shows a more clear left skewness with important tails to the left. In light of these observations, the use of weighted exposures per country of destination in the calculation of macroeconomic indicators seems to be reasonable.

