\begin{figure}[!hbt] % between [] you put options e.g. ht = here top
% \hspace*{-0.5cm}
 %\hspace*{-2cm}
\centering
%\rule{12.8cm}{7.2cm}
%\setlength{\abovecaptionskip}{5pt}
\makebox[\textwidth]{\includegraphics[width=0.6\paperwidth]{figure/CBDIndex1-1}}
%\vspace{-1cm}}
\caption{Cross-Border Diversification Index}
\label{CBDIndex1}
 %\vspace*{-3mm}
\end{figure}

Figure \ref{CBDIndex1} shows  the distribution of the Cross-Border Diversification Index, which represents the geographic concentration of the bank's original exposures, and its respective quantile-quantile plot. A Cross-Border Diversification Index(CBDI) close to its maximum (100) means that the bank's original exposures are very diversified across countries whereas an index close to zero means that the bank's original exposures are very concentrated on certain countries. In this figure, it is also observable  some flatness in the total distribution around a CBDI value of 50, which also provides some evidence in favor of the use of macroeconomic indicators weighted by the country of destination of exposures. Furthermore, Figure \ref{country_destination2} (appendix) illustrates the diversity  and the frequency of countries included in the  top 10 country of destiny for bank's exposures. In this regard, approximately 70 different countries of destiny around the wold  are presented in the data, although most frequent countries of destiny are located in Europe except for the United States, which shows a frequency of 9,6\%. 

    \FloatBarrier
    
\subsection{Country of Origin and the Single Supervisory Mechanism (SSM)}         

 
                    <<SSM,fig.width=6, fig.height=6, out.width='0.9\\linewidth',fig.show='hide'>>=
                      library(tidyverse)
                     path_data <-  file.path(getwd(), "Data")

                      load(paste(path_data,'all.RData', sep = "/"))
                      load(paste(path_data,'npl_hh.nfi.RData', sep = "/"))

                      library(plyr)
                      all1 <- all %>% mutate(Exposure= as.factor(Exposure))
                      all1$Exposure <- revalue(all1$Exposure,c("202" = "Financial Corporations", "301" = "Non-Financial Corporations","401" = "Households"))

                     SSM1 <- data.frame(all1 )%>% group_by(Year,SSM)%>% dplyr::count()
                      SSM2 <- SSM1%>% group_by(Year) %>%  summarise_at(vars(n),list(sum = sum))
                      SSM3 <-  SSM1 %>% left_join( SSM2, by=c("Year")) %>% mutate( pct2= n/sum)
                     # View(SSM3)

                    a <- ggplot(SSM3,
                            aes(x=Year, y=pct2,fill=SSM)) +
                       geom_col()+
                       xlab(" ") +
                       ylab(" ")+
                      # scale_fill_grey()+
                       theme_light()+
                       geom_text(aes(label = scales::percent(pct2)),
                                 position="stack",vjust=+2.3,col="black",size=2)+
                       #facet_wrap(~ Exposure, nrow = 3)+
                      # scale_fill_manual(values = c("lightslategray","lightblue"))+
                      # scale_fill_manual(values = c("royalblue2","lightgoldenrod1"))+
                       scale_fill_manual(values = c("royalblue2","goldenrod1"),name = " ", labels = c("Non-SSM", "SSM"))+
                       scale_y_continuous(label = scales::percent)+
                       theme(plot.title = element_text(hjust = 0.5,size=8))+
                        # theme(strip.background =element_rect(fill="lightblue4"))+
                        # theme(strip.text = element_text(colour = 'white'))+
                         theme(panel.background = element_rect(fill = "lightgray"),axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 30, hjust = 1, vjust = 1))+
                       theme(plot.caption = element_text(color = "black", face = "italic",hjust = 0.0))+
                       #theme(legend.position="right",legend.direction = "vertical",legend.spacing.x = unit(.5, 'cm'))+
                      theme(legend.position="bottom",legend.direction = "horizontal",legend.spacing.x = unit(.5, 'cm'))+
                       labs(caption = " ")+
                        ggtitle("SSM Membership (%)")

                    a1 <-   all  %>% ggplot(aes(x=SSM,y=NPL_ratio,colour=SSM)) +
                        geom_point(alpha=0.4,position = "jitter") +
                       scale_colour_manual(values = c("royalblue2","goldenrod1"),name = " ", labels = c("Non-SSM", "SSM"))+
                       xlab("Membership Status") +
                      ylab("NPL (%)")+
                      scale_y_continuous(label =  scales::percent_format(accuracy = .1,scale = 1))+
                     # theme(axis.text.x=element_blank(),axis.ticks.x = element_blank())+
                       theme(panel.background = element_rect(fill = "lightgray"),plot.title = element_text(hjust = 0.5,size=8))+
                     #theme(legend.position="right",legend.direction = "vertical",legend.spacing.x = unit(.5, 'cm'))+
                      theme(legend.position="bottom",legend.direction = "horizontal",legend.spacing.x = unit(.5, 'cm'))+
                       labs(caption = " ")+
                      ggtitle("SSM Membership and NPL(%)")
                        #facet_wrap(~var.name,scales = "free")+
                        # labs(x= "Seats Sold", y="Weekly Gross")+
                        #geom_smooth(method = "lm", se=TRUE, color="red", formula = y ~ x)


                     NSA1 <- data.frame(all1 )%>% group_by(nsa_label)%>% dplyr::count()
                     NSA2 <- data.frame(all1 )%>% dplyr::count()
                     NSA3 <-  NSA1  %>% mutate( pct2= n/ NSA2 [1,1])
                     # View(SSM3)

                     b <- ggplot(NSA3,
                            aes(x=nsa_label, y=pct2)) +
                       geom_bar(  stat='identity', position = 'stack',fill="lightblue4")+
                       xlab(" ") +
                       ylab(" ")+
                       # scale_fill_grey()+
                       theme_light()+
                       geom_text(aes(x=nsa_label, y=pct2,label = scales::percent(pct2,accuracy = .1,scale = 100), group =nsa_label),position=position_dodge(width = 1),vjust = -0.5, size = 2,col="black")+
                                # position="stack",vjust=+2.3,col="black",size=3)+
                       #facet_wrap(~ Exposure, nrow = 3)+
                        #scale_fill_manual(values = c("lightslategray","lightblue"))+
                       # scale_fill_manual(values = c("royalblue2","lightgoldenrod1"))+
                      # scale_fill_manual(values = c("royalblue2","goldenrod1"),name = " ", labels = c("NO", "YES"))+
                       scale_y_continuous(label =  scales::percent_format(accuracy = .1,scale = 100),breaks=seq(0,0.2,0.05),limits = c(0, .15)  )+
                       theme(plot.title = element_text(hjust = 0.5,size=8))+
                        theme(strip.background =element_rect(fill="lightblue4"))+
                        theme(strip.text = element_text(colour = 'white',size = 10))+
                       theme(panel.background = element_rect(fill = "lightgray"),axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 90, hjust = 1, vjust = 0))+
                       theme(plot.caption = element_text(color = "black", face = "italic",hjust = 0.0))+
                       #labs(caption = "Source: EBA Transparency Exercises, authors' calculations")+
                       ggtitle("Head Quarters(HQ)-Country of Origin (%)")


                     # ggarrange(a, a1,
                     #            ggarrange(b,ncol = 1,label=c("")),
                     #           ncol = 2,
                     #           labels = c(" ", " "))
                        library(ggpubr)
                     ggarrange(b,                                                 # First row with scatter plot
                              ggarrange(a, a1, ncol = 2, labels = c("", "")), # Second row with box and dot plots
                              nrow = 2,
                              labels = ""                                        # Labels of the scatter plot
                              )


                    @


\begin{figure}[!hbt] % between [] you put options e.g. ht = here top
% \hspace*{-0.5cm}
 %\hspace*{-2cm}
\centering
%\rule{12.8cm}{7.2cm}
%\setlength{\abovecaptionskip}{5pt}
\makebox[\textwidth]{\includegraphics[width=0.6\paperwidth]{figure/SSM-1}}
%\vspace{-1cm}}

\caption[Single Supervisory Mechanism (\%) \& Country of Origin (\%)]{ Single Supervisory Mechanism (\%) \& Country of Origin (\%)\\ {\footnotesize \textit{Source: EBA Transparency Exercises, authors' calculations}}}
\label{SSM}
 %\vspace*{-3mm}
\end{figure}

Figure \ref{SSM} provides basic information regarding the percentage distribution of the sample according to its SSM membership status during the period  2014-2019 as well as the distribution of banks per country of origin. Regarding the SSM membership most banks in the sample belong to EU countries, which are members of the Single Supervisory Mechanism, with a relation of approximately 80\%-20\% between SSM members and non-SSM. Despite these  asymmetries, there are enough observations for an empirical analysis of the effects of SSM membership on NPL ratios. In particular,  NPL ratios seems to be more dispersed for SSM members. The disproportional presence of SSM members with respect to non-SSM members in the sample  motivates the need of some caution  during the analysis of empirical results. The distribution of banks per country of origin is also asymmetric, with a higher concentration of banks located in Europe's largest economies.


  \FloatBarrier

\subsection{Descriptive Statistics and Correlation Diagrams}

Summary descriptive statistics are  reported in Table \ref{DescStats} (appendix). Particularly, the observation of important disparities in the metrics related to NPL both between and within exposure types must be noted. Correlation diagrams  are reported in figures  \ref{Corr.hh},\ref{Corr.nfi}, \ref{Corr.fi} (appendix). As observed in these figures, the correlation of the explanatory variables with NPL are similar between exposure types and, in general, low for the bank-specific variables while  increasing in the case of macroeconomic indicators. Interestingly, Gross public debt (gross\_public\_debt), Unemployment rates (unempl\_rate\_imf) and Recovery rates (Reco\_rate\_dol) show the highest correlation with NPL in the  macroeconomic variables under consideration. In regard to the bank-specific variables, the highest correlation is reported by leverage rates (LEVER\_tr\_ratio), Return on Risk-adjusted capital (RORAC) and Net interest margin (NIM\_tr). Additionally, a Variance Inflaction Factor (VIF)  test is performed  for each exposure type to measure the amount of multicollinearity in the context of multivariate regressions (see Table \ref{vif}) with NPL as response variable. As observed in Table \ref{vif}, the coefficients are  under 10 for all variables, providing some evidence  against the presence of multicollinearity  in a regression  with all these variables.

 
 

